ss instructor background?", "result_summary": "The presence of regular P-P intervals supports a sinus origin of rhythm abnormalities, aligning with the high likelihood of primary sinus bradycardia in an otherwise healthy young woman.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "weak_against", "B2": "moderate_for"}}, {"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 2, "bundle_size": 3, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Atrioventricular Conduction Abnormality? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "result_summary": "Normal vital signs and youth status create a weak challenge to intrinsic AV pathophysiology as the primary diagnosis, maintaining neutrality for other branches.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "weak_against", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 2, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the ECG in Figure A exhibit consistent PR intervals with intermittently dropped QRS complexes, as expected in Mobitz type II AV block compared to sinus bradycardia?", "result_summary": "ECG findings support Mobitz II AV block over sinus bradycardia based on consistent PR intervals with dropped beats.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "moderate_for", "B2": "weak_against"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 1, "bundle_size": 2, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are there any ECG findings in Figure A that would argue against intrinsic AV pathology (e.g., normal QRS despite intermittent blocking suggests no structural disease)?", "result_summary": "Normal QRS morphology argues against intrinsic AV structural disease, indirectly supporting reactive arrhythmia (B3)", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "weak_against", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "strong_for"}}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.3302752293577982, "B2": 0.5779816513761468, "B3": 0.09174311926605505}, {"B1": 0.3448275862068966, "B2": 0.4827586206896552, "B3": 0.1724137931034483, "B1.1": 0.05387931034482758, "B1.2": 0.16163793103448273, "B1.3": 0.10775862068965517, "B1.4": 0.021551724137931032}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No immediate instability markers identified (airway, breathing, circulation stable; no uncontrolled bleeding or herniation risk). Mobitz type II and sinus bradycardia remain differentials, but patient is asymptomatic with normal vital signs.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading branch B2 (Sinus bradycardia) has posterior=0.4827 (below 0.75). B1.3 (Mobitz II) retains posterior=0.1077 with danger=0.8. Discriminators (e.g., syncope history, family conduction disorders) still available to resolve ambiguity despite partial ECG analysis."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.65, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.4827586206896552, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_vignette": "A 24-year-old woman presents to clinic for a routine annual exam. She has generally been feeling well, but notes feeling intermittent palpitations over the past few months. Her past medical history is unremarkable and she is not currently taking any medications. She denies drinking alcohol, smoking cigarettes, or using recreational drugs. She attributes her palpitations to recently drinking more caffeine, but would like to obtain an electrocardiogram since her symptoms are worrisome and affecting her ability to concentrate on her career as a fitness instructor. Her temperature is 36.9°C (98.4°F), blood pressure is 116/76 mmHg, pulse is 55/min, respirations are 12/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Her electrocardiogram is shown in Figure A.", "static_question": "Which