lar Block (Complete Heart Block):\n  Favours first:  feeling of skipped heartbeats\n  Favours second: bradycardia, heart palpitations, rapid breathing, tiredness\n\nRelated to First Degree Atrioventricular Block: atrioventricular block (disease), genetic cardiac rhythm disease\n\nRelated to Second Degree Atrioventricular Block (Mobitz Type I - Wenckebach): atrioventricular block (disease), genetic cardiac rhythm disease\n\nRelated to Second Degree Atrioventricular Block (Mobitz Type II - Non-Wenckebach): atrioventricular block (disease), genetic cardiac rhythm disease\n\nRelated to Third Degree Atrioventricular Block (Complete Heart Block): atrioventricular block (disease), genetic cardiac rhythm disease\n\n[RAG context for 'A 24-year-old woman presents to clinic for a routine annual exam' (score=0.69):]\n  Source: Electrocardiographic diagnosis of atrial tachycardia: classification, P-wave morphology, and differential diagnosis with other supraventricular tachycardias. > Deterrence and Patient Education\n  Risk factors for arrhythmia are similar to risk factors for developing heart disease. A heart-healthy lifestyle with a well-balanced diet, exercising and refraining from tobacco products is essential \n[RAG context for 'A 24-year-old woman presents to clinic for a routine annual exam' (score=0.69):]\n  Source: The Impact of Caffeine and Coffee on Human Health. > Monitoring\n  It is prudent for the clinician considering stimulant therapy for any condition for which it is indicated to perform a careful assessment of the patient by using a targeted cardiac history (eg, patien\n[RAG context for 'She has generally been feeling well' (score=0.70):]\n  Source: The Impact of Caffeine and Coffee on Human Health. > Monitoring\n  It is prudent for the clinician considering stimulant therapy for any condition for which it is indicated to perform a careful assessment of the patient by using a targeted cardiac history (eg, patien\n[RAG context for 'She has generally been feeling well' (score=0.70):]\n  Source: Overview of Basic Mechanisms of Cardiac Arrhythmia. > Deterrence and Patient Education\n  Patients should be taught that reentrant arrhythmias are caused by a problem with the heart’s conduction mechanism, which coordinates the beating of the heart. They should be informed of the mechanism\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  intermittent palpitations attributed to increased caffeine intake → Caffeine-induced reactive arrhythmia → Reactive Arrhythmia with Caffeine Sensitivity\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for caffeine-induced reactive arrhythmia through EKG monitoring and caffeine challenge testing before structural block diagnosis\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
